weave-knee trial v2
fanout: 5
seed: 1
weaver: claude-sonnet-5
judge: claude-sonnet-5
quality: 4
narrative:
  Ostsax runs as a closed loop of five modules, each holding data only briefly and each answerable only through the others. Griquen, the cache, evicts anything left unclaimed after 7 ticks, refuses new writes while its sweep runs, and answers reads solely from its newest snapshot; it publishes its sweep verdicts to Pambra. Pambra, the sampler, keeps only digests of what it carries, never bodies, evicts unclaimed items after 7 ticks like Griquen, refuses writes during its own sweep, and reports missed heartbeats onward to Brabra. Brabra, the warden, evicts after just 6 ticks — the tightest window in the system — refuses writes mid-sweep, and will act only once 5 of its peers countersign, making every eviction it streams to Keltol a quorum-gated decision rather than a unilateral one. Keltol, the scheduler, also keeps digests rather than bodies, also refuses writes during its sweep, and retries a failed handoff at most once before parking it; it hands its sealed ledger to Tolkel. Tolkel, the throttle, keeps digests only, answers reads from its newest snapshot only (mirroring Griquen), evicts unclaimed work after 9 ticks, and delegates its overflow back to Griquen — closing the cycle: Griquen to Pambra to Brabra to Keltol to Tolkel to Griquen again.

  Because the loop is closed, no module's guarantees stand alone: overflow that leaves Tolkel eventually returns to Tolkel by way of the other four. And because Griquen, Pambra, Brabra, Keltol, and Tolkel each refuse new writes while sweeping, a write attempting to traverse the full loop has five independent points at which it can stall — the system offers no moment provably free of write-refusal somewhere along the cycle. The loop's data-retention floor is set by its strictest member: Brabra's 6-tick eviction is shorter than Griquen's and Pambra's 7 and Tolkel's 9, so nothing survives the full circuit longer than 6 ticks, however generous the other stages are. That floor is itself conditional — Brabra only evicts, and only streams to Keltol, once 5 peers countersign — and even a countersigned handoff can die: Keltol parks it permanently after a single failed retry, meaning the cycle Griquen–Pambra–Brabra–Keltol–Tolkel is closed by design but not guaranteed to complete on any given pass.
